{"answer":"For n=1, the sum is 1=1^2. Assume 1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2. Adding the next odd number gives\n\\[\n1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)+(2n+1)=n^2+2n+1=(n+1)^2.\n\\]\nThus the identity holds for n+1; by induction, it holds for every positive integer n.","approaches":[{"description":"Mathematical induction using the fact that the next odd number after 2n-1 is 2n+1.","id":"induction-proof","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The base case and induction step establish the stated identity for all positive integers."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by induction in steps base-case, induction-step, and induction-conclusion.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"sum-of-odds","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every positive integer n, 1+3+5+...+(2n-1)=n^2.","step_ids":["base-case","induction-step","induction-conclusion"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"base-case","justification":"At n=1, the left-hand side is 1 and 1=1^2.","statement":"The identity holds for n=1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"induction-step","justification":"Assuming the identity at n, append the next term 2n+1: n^2+(2n+1)=n^2+2n+1=(n+1)^2.","statement":"If the identity holds for a positive integer n, then it holds for n+1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["base-case","induction-step"],"id":"induction-conclusion","justification":"The principle of mathematical induction applies to the base case and implication established above.","statement":"The identity holds for every positive integer n."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
